The charge is for "this service" (distribution).  That includes whatever
overheads the distributor wants it to cover (including but not limited
to production and shipping).  Only the source *has* to be made available
for a sensible fee.

The competition ensures that a distributor *won't* charge that much for
such a CD, but that's not the same as "can't".

>  I stand behind my point. Free software is just that *free*. 

Even without exploring the libre/gratis distinction, that's quite
different to your original claim that "you *can't* charge money for
bash".  Although you're probably going to argue about the definition of
each word in order to claim that you were factually correct, your
wording was misleading - many linux-on-a-CD distributors can and do
charge money for bash (along with all the other software in the
distribution, including nethack).

If you'd included some kind of qualifier, like "you can't _just_ charge
money for bash" (because distributing it brings other obligations) or
"you can't charge money for bash and expect to make a significant profit
from it", I wouldn't have said anything.

